%erg v1
Title: Define and enforce text file encoding stance (BOM, EOL, UTF-8)
Created: 2026-05-11
Author: MinhHaDuong
Tag: needs-human

--- log ---
2026-05-11T20:30Z claude open — surfaced during audit lint session; spec says UTF-8 LF but validator does not enforce
2026-05-12T08:00Z MinhHaDuong decide — option B (tolerant parse, warn on check); normalize CRLF→LF, strip BOM during parse, warn in erg check

--- body ---
## Problem

The spec says "UTF-8, LF line endings" but the validator does not
enforce either. parseErgBytes splits on \n and silently swallows \r\n
(the \r sticks to the end of header values). No BOM detection. No
encoding validation.

## Options

A. **Strict**: reject BOM, reject \r\n, reject non-UTF-8 bytes. Catches
   problems early but breaks Windows-edited files.

B. **Tolerant**: strip BOM if present, normalize \r\n -> \n during
   parse, accept valid UTF-8 only. Postel's law (which the spec already
   invokes). erg check warns about BOM and \r\n so user can fix editor
   settings.

C. **Ignore**: document "UTF-8, LF" and don't enforce. Current de facto
   behavior. Risk: header values silently carry \r, which breaks string
   comparisons downstream.

## Decision needed

Author picks a stance. Recommendation: option B (tolerant parse, warn
on check). The \r-in-header-values bug is real — CRLF files will have
Title values ending in \r, which means FilenameID comparisons and tag
matching can silently fail.
